        public void PUT_IndexOfContract_NoAssumptions([PexMethodUnderTest] ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            var Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            var Old_x = x;
            var Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            var Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            var New_Ret = arrList.IndexOf(x);

            var New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            var New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            var New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            // An assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ If the return value is non-negative, list counts of new and old array are equal 
            if (New_Ret >=0){
                Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount);
            }

            if (New_Ret == Old_arrListLastIndexOfX){ // @@@ If the item last index of old array list is the same as the new array list index of the item, then check if new array list contains this item 
                Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListContainsX && New_arrListContainsX);
            }

            if (Old_arrListContainsX){ //@@@ if old array list contains x, check if x is not in the new array list 
                Assert.IsFalse(New_arrListContainsX);
            }
            //@@@ If the item last index of the old array list is the same as the new array list index of item, and they have different capacity, then the count of the new array list cannot be larger than the old array list. 
            if (New_arrListCount != Old_arrListCount && Old_arrListLastIndexOfX == New_arrListLastIndexOfX){
                Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListCount <= Old_arrListCount);
            }
        }
    }
}

The test class contains methods where contract assertions are made for each of the methods. First, an assumption is made for the method where we can assume that the method will work correctly. We then make an unverified assumption for a method under test.

We use Precondition.IsTrue() to establish a precondition that our test will only be executed if it is true.

Then, we set up some state before and after the method under test: the array list counts, the item 'x', whether the array list contains 'x', and the last index of 'x' in the array list. The method returns the index of 'x'.

We use Observe.ValueForViewing() to provide output for our code coverage report and debugging.

Then, the assertions are made. To keep the example small, only one assertion is shown here. 

The test should be modified by adding new assertions for each possible scenario. The most critical scenario is asserting the change in the state of the ArrayList after it is run in the production code. If the method returns an index that is non-negative, and the Count of the ArrayList before and after the method call is the same, then the method worked correctly according to its requirements." 
}


